body:
The "10 seconds to 5 minutes" range creates a 30× variance that prevents developers from writing reliable code. Given this feature's goal of simplifying async work during unload (per explainer: easier than service workers), developers need predictable behavior to estimate task completion times and handle edge cases.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('pagehide', () => {
  const worker = new SharedWorker('compress-upload.js', { extendedLifetime: true });
  worker.port.postMessage({ data: largeDataset });

  // Developer cannot reliably:
  // - Show accurate UI ("saving... may take 10s? or 5min?")
  // - Design tasks to fit within timeout
  // - Debug timeout-related failures across browsers
});
```

Could we narrow the recommended range and align with service worker precedent:
> A typical value for the extended lifetime shared worker timeout
> should be consistent with the user agent's service worker lifetime policy when idle, 
> typically in the range of 20-60 seconds. Values significantly outside this range may 
> cause interoperability issues and unpredictable developer experience.

To aid cross-browser development, it'd be great if we could consider exposing the timeout value via JavaScript which is similar to how developers can detect other implementation-defined limits.

Given this feature aims to improve DX over service workers (per explainer: simpler registration, blob: URL support), option mismatch errors should provide clear diagnostics to maintain that DX advantage.

```javascript
// library-a.js
new SharedWorker('analytics.js', { extendedLifetime: true });

// library-b.js (different file, no knowledge of library-a)
new SharedWorker('analytics.js', { extendedLifetime: false });
// → onerror fires, but developer doesn't know:
//    - Which option failed
//    - What the existing value is
//    - How to fix it
```

It'd be great if we can populate the error message with the conflicting option name, existing value, and requested value:
> "Failed to construct 'SharedWorker': option 'extendedLifetime' mismatch (existing: true, requested: false)"
